{"text": " undergone a few reorganizations since its independence from France in 1960.\n\n 1960: Ivory Coast gains independence\n\nThe Ivory Coast gained its independence from France on August 7, 1960. The young nation needed a military to protect its interests. The military was called L'Armée Nationale (National Army), and its purpose was to defend the nation against potential threats from within or without. During this time, the nation did not have enough money to develop a fully operational military so it relied on the French military for defense. The military was led by President Félix Houphouët-Boigny.\n\n 1970: President Houphouet-Boigny requests that France withdraw its military forces.\n\nOn 28 November 1970, Félix Houphouet-Boigny asks France to withdraw all its military forces and personnel from the Ivory Coast. On 1 January 1971, Ivory Coast's defense system passes from the control of the French military to that of the Ivory Coast government.\n\n 1990s–2000s: The First and Second Ivorian Civil War\n\nWhen the civil war began in 1990, Ivory Coast's military was greatly underprepared for the challenges it would face. In 1993, the armed forces were estimated to have approximately 18,500 active personnel. The nation's military forces lacked modern equipment. The Ivorian military also lacked modern tactics and doctrines, which made them vulnerable to a coup d'etat on 25 October 1999. In 2002, the First Ivorian Civil War breaks out between the government and several groups in opposition. These groups include the New Forces, a militant group led by the former President Laurent Gbagbo. By the end of the conflict, in 2003, the military is estimated to have approximately 27,100 active personnel.\n\nThe Ivorian military and the New Forces signed a peace agreement on January 1, 2003, which led to a ceasefire.  The New Forces then disbanded and was replaced by the Forces Républicaines de Côte d'Ivoire (FRCI), the armed forces of the transitional government led by President Gbagbo.  The Forces Nouvelles (FN) also disbanded and formed a political party called Rassemblement des Républicains.  Gbagbo attempted to reform the military and integrate elements of the New Forces into the new military. The Ivorian military is also being reorganized into six military regions with the military region", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 512}
