idely used home computer of the 1990s.\n\nThe A500 was launched as the next generation of the 1985 Amiga 1000 and the 1986 Amiga 2000, to directly compete with the IBM PC. At $1,000, the 500 had a higher cost of entry compared to the Amiga 2000 at $999 and the Macintosh II at $1,800. However, the 500 had superior graphics and sound.\n\nDuring the transition from the Commodore 64 to the Amiga, the Amiga 500 was used as the base of the Commodore Amiga CD32 console, a more compact machine than the 500. The A500 was replaced by the Amiga 1200 (not to be confused with the Commodore 1200 computer system also released in the same year) in the early 1990s. According to the \"Commodore 64: A Visual Compendium of the Worlds Most Popular Machine\" book, the Amiga 500 was \"sold in enough quantities to rival the success of the C64\".\n\nIn 2010, Amiga Inc., which obtained the rights to the Amiga brand from 2011 to 2012, announced the Amiga 500 20th Anniversary Edition, a high-end kit version based on the original A500 with a Commodore A1200 motherboard and several new internal components including a 100 MHz 68060 processor, 8 MB of RAM, and a Commodore-branded IDE interface. The updated system was released in July 2011, with prices set at US$999 and £999. In July 2012, it was officially renamed to the Amiga500/60.\n\nIn 2013, A-Eon Technology Ltd., the new licensor and distributor of the Amiga brand, announced the Amiga 500 Mini, a modern plug-and-play machine designed to match the original 500 in size and functionality. The A500 Mini was released in November 2017.\n\nHistory\n\nIn the early 1980s, Commodore began working on a series of computers that would use the Amiga chipset, based on the concept of \"bitplanes\". In 1986, the Amiga 2000 was released, as the top-of-the-line machine to replace the Amiga 1000. However, it failed to displace the Amiga 1000, which continued to be more powerful.\n\nIn", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 3, "token_count": 512}
